So, $\dfrac{\sin\theta}
{\sin^2 \theta + \cos^2 \theta} =
\dfrac{\sin\theta}{1} = \sin\theta$

So, $\dfrac{\csc\theta}
{\sin^2 \theta + \cos^2 \theta} =
\dfrac{\csc\theta}{1} = \csc\theta$

Plugging into our expression, we get
$\qquad
\dfrac{1 - \cos^2\theta}{\sin^2\theta}
=
\dfrac{\sin^2\theta}{\sin^2\theta}
$

Plugging into our expression, we get
$\qquad
\dfrac{1 - \cos^2\theta}{\sin^2\theta}
=
\dfrac{\sin^2\theta}{\sin^2\theta}
$

Dividing both sides by $\cos^2\theta$ , we get
$\qquad \dfrac{\sin^2\theta}{\cos^2\theta}
+ \dfrac{\cos^2\theta}{\cos^2\theta}
= \dfrac{1}{\cos^2\theta}$
$\qquad \tan^2\theta + 1
= \sec^2\theta$

Plugging into our expression, we get
$\qquad
\dfrac{\tan^2\theta + 1}{\sec^2\theta}
=
\dfrac{\sec^2\theta}{\sec^2\theta}
$

Plugging into our expression, we get
$\qquad
\dfrac{\sec^2\theta-1}{\tan^2\theta}
=
\dfrac{\tan^2\theta}{\tan^2\theta}
$

Plugging into our expression, we get
$\qquad
\dfrac{\csc^2\theta-1}{\csc^2\theta}
=
\dfrac{\cot^2\theta}{\csc^2\theta}
$

Dividing both sides by $\sin^2\theta$ , we get
$\qquad \dfrac{\sin^2\theta}{\sin^2\theta}
+ \dfrac{\cos^2\theta}{\sin^2\theta}
= \dfrac{1}{\sin^2\theta}$
$\qquad 1 + \cot^2\theta
= \csc^2\theta$

Dividing both sides by $\sin^2\theta$ , we get
$\qquad \dfrac{\sin^2\theta}{\sin^2\theta}
+ \dfrac{\cos^2\theta}{\sin^2\theta}
= \dfrac{1}{\sin^2\theta}$
$\qquad 1 + \cot^2\theta
= \csc^2\theta$

Plugging into our expression, we get
$\qquad
\dfrac{1 + \cot^2\theta}{\cot^2\theta}
=
\dfrac{\csc^2\theta}{\cot^2\theta}
$

